      "writtenOn": "2026-10-08T08:35:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Optional, may be worth exploring: these probes are independent but run sequentially (the findAll at line 62), so wall-clock is the sum of all round trips. If the shared http client is thread-safe, a small bounded pool (~4–8, CompletableFuture) would cut that to roughly the slowest single probe. Probably unnecessary given the daily cache, and there\u0027s no batch endpoint — countTokens is per-model — so per-model probing stays regardless.",
      "revId": "70eddd659961ab67d23e57733eb3dc3d4d21dde2",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6a3f942a_078eb862",
        "filename": "ai/ai-review-agent-gemini-1.0.groovy",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 74,
      "author": {
        "id": 1025452
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-08T11:40:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I would keep it sequential. The shared client (AiHttpClientProvider.createDefaultHttpClient) keeps Apache\u0027s default PoolingHttpClientConnectionManager limit of 2 connections per route, so parallel probes would gain about 2x at most, and while they run they would take both connections from live review calls to the same host. Not worth the extra code for a once-a-day cost.",
